WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760
Skate or die, psych up for five of the raddest skating events ever!

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:28.960
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:30.900
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:32.320
And I'm Sam.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:39.240
Guys, there's a saying in video gaming called EA Sports.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:40.380
It's in the game.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.040
Do you guys know what I'm talking about?

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:43.780
Yes, that's an old adage.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.240
I'm not sure the origin, that's lost to time.

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:48.980
That is lost to time.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:51.800
In fact, some say that that was said before video games.

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:56.600
Electronic Arts actually just said, like, wow, that's such a familiar saying to us, we're

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.120
just going to include it in our sports games.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:02.340
It's what kicked off the original Olympics in Greco-Roman time.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.720
Yeah. It's what's for dinner.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:07.300
Nice. Okay, we're all doing very good.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:08.660
We're all on hype levels here.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:14.680
And the reason I bring that up at all is because Skate or Die was Electronic Arts' first in-house

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:15.680
game ever.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:19.360
You know, like, the first developed by full-time employees.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.020
All prior EA titles had been done by independent contractors.

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:26.940
So this is the people that brought you Mass Effect, right?

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:29.660
Not really, though, because they published that one, too.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:30.940
But, like, you get the idea.

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:32.580
At one point, they made the games.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.400
So, wow, I didn't even realize this was Electronic Arts.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.140
Like, I didn't even see that in the little footer down there.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:44.500
Well, you were expecting the E to come on to the left-hand side and then the A.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.240
Yeah. But it didn't happen.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:48.880
No, but, yeah, this is an EA game.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:56.440
The thing is, though, Sean, more likely the reason you didn't see it on the game anywhere

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:59.660
is because Konami did the porting here.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.340
So do we judge this as a EA game making a video game for the first time?

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:07.280
Or do we judge Konami because we love Konami games?

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.200
Or is it both of them are responsible?

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.560
I guess this is a Konami game.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:13.380
I like Sam's idea.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.060
Why not both? Everyone should be held.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:16.640
In fact, Nintendo, right?

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:18.260
They gave the seal of quality.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:20.920
We should always hold them responsible every episode.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:22.940
I don't think that means anything.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:25.940
The seal. So not yet.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:27.800
